Date: Sun, 24 Oct 93 19:43:07 GMT
From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Subject: QUANTUM: Many Minds Interpretation (was: free will)

Paul Cisek says in X-Message-Number: #93-10-697
>Which philosophical interpretations do I have to choose from?  You seem to
>suggest that the only choices are: Copenhagen - consciousness mystically
>affects physical reality; Many Worlds - consciousness selects which of many
>parallel realities it will drift through.  Must we accept that these are the
>only interpretations possible?

There are some others, but they each have their own set of problems.  And
there might be other so far undiscovered solutions.  In the thread from
last March, Mike Price listed a few interpretations:

> I have a simple answer to mysticism or supernaturalism in physics: the
> bin.  As for QM/Schrodinger's Cat - there are a number of
> interpretations (and different categorisations possible):
> -    hidden variables (Bohm)
> -    many-worlds (Everett)
> -    sum-over-histories (Feynman)
> -    participatory model (Wheeler)
> -    Copenhagenism (Bohr plus others)
> -    complementarity (Bohr)

Hidden variables was an alternative to quantum theory, developed by
Einstein's followers.  It proposed an underlying reality more in line
with the "local realistic" model; that when you create a pair of photons
for instance each of them has a particular actual polarization as soon
as they are created.  Standard quantum theory said that they did not
have a polarization until it was measured.  But hidden variables was
shown to be false by the Aspect experiments -- its predictions were
inaccurate.  As for Bohm, he thinks that 'everything is connected to
everything else', and we can only explain things like human
consciousness by taking a holistic approach.

Sum-over-histories is a rather obscure model that I don't seem to have
any information on.  I believe it suggests whenever a particle goes from
A to B it takes all possible routes, travelling backwards in time whenever
it goes from B to A.  And this could give the right results if the number
of times it went over each route was in accordance with the probability
amplitude.  But the particle has to be quite smart.  It has to start out
on the first route after searching for the quantum complex amplitude for
that route in a look-up table, working out the squared modulus and
setting a counter which it decrements each time it gets to B.  (And it's
not troubled by time running backwards!)  On returning to A AND seeing a
zero, it then has to work out the next route, compute the probability
again and set the counter, etc, until it's completed all routes,
remembering which routes have been completed.  (This is a theory which
could be cheap on particles but long in assumptions.)

Or is it the hoary old chestnut "the oscillatory universe"?  This wouldn't
allow for open universes, and rather than converting very unlikely events
into certainties appears to do the very opposite in requiring life on
Earth to arise and evolve to human level an infinite number of times in
succession.  In short, Many Worlds or Many Minds is infinitely more likely. 

John Wheeler's participatory universe is a model where answers do not
exist until the questions have been asked, or the universe does not
exist unless we are looking at it.  His idea gives the same results as
Many Worlds, and I think he's right in a sense when he says that it's
only the observers in a universe who notice what is going on that
impart a tangible reality (to the past and present).  It's like reality
itself evolves and gradually coalesces.  So before we'd seen Pluto,
humans occupied universes where it both did and didn't exist.  But I
think this is just suggesting the consequences of MW/MM but without
providing any mechanism to explain it. 
  
As for complementarity, this was just one of the Copenhagen concepts.  It
said that matter was supposed to have the dual property of being both
particle and wave.  The "Copenhagen interpretation" combined several
ideas: complementarity, probability, uncertainty, and the notion that the
observer could affect the system by observation.  

If anyone knows of a better interpretation, now's your chance!

>I never suggested that the Copenhagen interpretation must be right, only that
>the Many Worlds makes Occam wince.

Actually, even the critics seem to think that Many Worlds is not really
something to make William of Ockham turn in his grave.  It's been
described as "cheap on assumptions but expensive on universes".  But
when you consider that universes are not merely cheap, but are an
exception to the rule "there's no such thing as a free lunch", it wins
on assumptions _and_ economy.  (I've just been looking this up, and it
seems that "entia non sunt multiplicanda praeter necessitatem" didn't
actually appear in William's works, so it could have been assumptions,
not entities, that he advocated not multiplying unnecessarily.)

>> [short description of the Aspect experiments]

>I'm sorry, but I don't think I understand the setup...  Is this right?:
>
>An atom produces two photons of opposite polarization.  One passes through a
>random gate that sends it either to a "up" detector or a "down" detector, the
>other goes straight.  Regardless of the choice made at the switch, one of the
>detectors goes off even if only one photon went through the switch - thus
>suggesting that a single photon can have both polarizations.  The other photon
>is measured to have the opposite polarization.  Thus the random choice made at
>the switch affects both the photon that went through it and the one that
>didn't.  (Am I getting this right?  Your description was a bit unclear).

In the Aspect experiments, each photon beam went through a random gate
sending it to one of two differently aligned filters plus detectors.  The
switching of each gate would not have been perfectly random, but control
of one was independent of the other.  Because switching occured after the
photons' emission and before travel through the gate, correlations could
not have occured by the photons somehow being 'aware' of the setup as
they were created.  So how did the predictions of QM differ from the
local realistic model?

Bell's theorem showed that the actual quantum probabilities could not be
derived from any local realistic model.  It's normally expressed as
it applies to spin one-half particles such as electrons and protons, so
I'll stick to this convention for the moment.  Here's a variation where
you stick to measurements in one plane:

Take a pair of spin one-half particles which have been created together
such that conservation of angular momentum requires their 'spins' to be
opposite.  The particle that went off to the left is either going to be
tested for an 'up' (0 deg.) spin or a 'right' (90 deg.) spin.  The right
bearing particle is either checked for 'diagonally up and right' (45 deg.)
spin or a 135 deg. spin.  Let's say we start with the measurers set at
90 deg. for the L particle and 45 deg. for R.  The quantum mechanical
probability of getting agreement between two measurements (either 2
'yeses' or 2 'noes') is given by 0.5(1 + cos theta) where theta is the
angle between measurements where spin is equal.  So for opposite spin we
need theta = 180 - 45 deg., which gives 0.1464 probability, and this is
what would be obtained after a long series of measurements.

Now, if there is no "ghostly" superluminal or "holistic" connection to
influence the R measurements from the L setting, we could have set L to
test for 'up' or 0 deg. spin instead, and the run of results from R would
have been exactly the same.  The difference between both measurements is
still 45 degrees, so we should still get under 15% correlations as above.
Next assume the L measurement was still set at 90 deg., and it was R that
had been changed to 135 degrees.  We get the old L results but a second
set for R, again with under 15% correlation.  The final combination has L
set to 0 degrees and R set to 135.  This time the correlation should be
0.5(1 + cos(180-135)) = 85.35%.  Since the first three combinations all
have correlations of under 15% it follows that the maximum correlation
in the final L=0, R=135 combination of settings must still be under
3 * 15%, or 45%, provided the choice of measurement on one cannot
influence the results obtained from the other.  But hey presto! the
correlation was higher than it 'should' have been, so the notion that
there are no superluminal correlations is false.  (The hidden variable
supporters need something like a linear cos law, a bit like if the tan
law had been linear all the way then Mercury's perihelion would have
been o.k. for Newton.)

Tests have been carried out with protons which gave the quantum 
correlations.  Most of the tests such as Aspect's have been with photons,
and the principle is the same, but for spin one particles the equivalent
correlation probability occurs at half the angle.  Change the measurement
alignment for a photon by 45 degrees and there's a 50% chance of a
correlation, equivalent to 90 degrees for a spin one-half particle. 
(The formula cos squared theta can be used for photons.) 

The local realistic or hidden-variable model says that the pair of
photons already have a polarization before they've been measured, and
they are only correlated as expected from conservation of angular
momentum.  QM says that the entire state is a superposition of all
possible spin states for the first particle together with the
corresponding spin state for its twin.  The Copenhagenists claim that
there is no objective underlying reality at the quantum level and end
up with minds being able to "collapse wavefunctions" and regular
violations of Special Relativity.  

What Copenhagenists fail to recognise is that reality is a lot more
objective that they admit.  If you take all the possible permutations
of reality where a given individual's brain is duplicated and superposed
at the same point in time and space, all of these brains have no option
but to share the same consciousness and memories.  To assume otherwise
would require some non-material substance that differed between brains
that were physically identical.  So these brains are all the same mind
and the same individual, existing in a "multiverse", a myriad of
individual universes which are distinguished physically in ways which
are unknown to the individual at that time.  And it is easily possible
for a few atoms in the brain to be slightly rearranged, or for the brains
to be displaced by a few microns without causing a divergence into
separate minds.  In fact, in some universes, the individual's brain is a
simulation, "aware" of fabrications like "the Milky Way". 
 
But as soon as the brains physically diverge sufficiently to differentiate
consciousness and memories, they have no option but to become separate
minds in separate multiverses.  To maintain co-existence would require
telepathy!  So before splitting a scientist's mind would occupy a
multiverse where a pair of particles existed in a quantum linear
superposition of possible spin states, each possible combination being
a myriad of universes in its own right.  After the measurements are made
and the results observed, the scientist's mind splits into each possible
sub-division and go happily on their separate paths.  No matter which
direction of spin was tested for, as soon as the experimenter becomes
aware of the result his minds separate and he ends up in some possible
permutation.  As soon as he becomes aware of the state of the leftwards
travelling particles, the rightwards twins are forced to "jump" to the
appropriate state because the scientist's mind has already "jumped".
 
I expect Many Minds / Many Worlds is often rejected by people because
they mistakenly think of the "universe" having to somehow "split" every
time a choice arises at the quantum level.  It's far better to think of
each possible permutation of reality or universe as already having a
separate objective existence.  If they didn't, they'd be the same
universe.  Universes don't split, but minds certainly diverge for the
simple reason that "spirits" and "telepathy" are both non-scientific
fallacious concepts.  The sum total of existence, the omniverse, is
filled with all these minds, each occupying their very own personal
multiverse at each instant.

The "local realistic" view is actually quite correct.  There are real
things that exist regardless of whether we observe them, it is legitimate
to draw general conclusions from consistent observations and experiments,
and no influence can propagate faster than lightspeed.

