Tim* writes:
>If I've gotten this wrong, hopefully someone
>like David Burns will correct me, but the neoclassical seems to be
>characterized by "comparative statics," the comparison and contrast of
>static "pictures" of markets.  The Austrian school is a.k.a. the
>"market-process" school because it relies upon analysis of markets as
>dynamic processes, more like movies than still pictures.
>
>Tim Starr - Renaissance Now!

Rest assured I'll correct you even if you're right!-)

Quibble - comparative statics is not 'the comparison and contrast of
static "pictures" of markets' but analysis and comparison of static
equilibria. A snapshot of a market might show it in equilibrium or in flux,
just as a photo of a projectile could show it in flight or at rest. Static
analysis can compare equilibria, but ignores the path between equilibria.
Only equilibria, where the various market forces are perfectly balanced and
all markets clear, count. Market process types are unanimous in thinking
that this is not the whole story, but do not agree on whether there is any
value in it at all. Some consider it a useful approximation or a starting
point for analysis, some think that the economy is far enough away from
equilibrium to make the concept irrelevant. 

Another good reason the Austrian school is a.k.a. the "market-process"
school is because it's too confusing calling someone born in Texas an
Austrian. People will think he's from Austin and can't spell.

