Extropians,

This question was asked of Duncan:

    How about Canada? Or is it too "close" to the USG?

To which Duncan replied:

    Canada is not a bad choice.  Every bank offers CA$ and
    US$ accounts.  It is a little close but if you aren't a
    big-time crook...

Actually, Canada is a pretty good choice.  For one thing, Canada
has an extremely low profile.  One of the reasons is that it has
no explicit bank secrecy laws.  Also, the IRS and Revenue Canada
have a *nominal* agreement to share information.  All this means
in practice, though, is that they will share any information the
*have in their possession*.

The kicker is that Revenue Canada exempts banks from reporting on
accounts which (a) are denominated in currency other than the
Canadian dollar, (b) contain no funds from Canadians sources, and
(c) are held in the name of non-Canadians.

If an American opens a US dollar account with funds that come
from the US, the bank will make no reports about it to Revenue
Canada.  What Revenue Canada doesn't know, it isn't able, nor
obliged, to report to the IRS.  Of course, other techniques may
be used to increase this strong but informal privacy protection.
One example:  A Caribbean company (owned by the holders of its
bearer certificates) opens a US dollar account with money from
the United Kingdom.  Pretty Good Privacy.

